WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.960
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.950
Artificial neural networks are computer programs
that try to approximate what the human brain

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:16.059
does to solve problems like recognizing objects
in images. In this piece of work, the authors

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:21.199
analyze the properties of these neural networks
and try to unveil what exactly makes them

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:26.550
think that a paper towel is a paper towel,
and, building on this knowledge, try to fool

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.850
these programs.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:29.670
Let's have a look at this example.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:35.479
One can grab this input image, and this noise
pattern, and add these two images together

00:00:35.479 --> 00:00:40.909
similarly as one would add two numbers together.
The operation yields the image you see here.

00:00:40.909 --> 00:00:45.989
I think it's fair to say that the difference
is barely perceptible for the human eye. Not

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:51.399
so much for neural networks, because the input
image we started with is classified correctly

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:56.760
as a bus, and the image that you see on the
right is classified as an ostrich.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:03.979
In simple terms, bus + noise equals an ostrich.
The two images look almost exactly the same,

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:09.390
but the neural networks see them quite differently.
We call these examples adversarial examples

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.939
because they are designed to fool these image
recognition programs.

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:18.369
In machine learning research, there are common
datasets to test different classification

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:24.369
techniques on, one of best known example is
the MNIST handwriting dataset. It is a basically

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:30.079
a bunch of images depicting handwritten numbers
that machine learning algorithms have to recognize.

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:36.049
Long ago, this used to be a difficult problem,
but nowadays, any half-decent algorithm can

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:41.240
guess the numbers correctly more than 99%
of the time after learning for just a few

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.409
seconds.

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:48.469
Now we'll see that these adversarial examples
are not created by chance: if we add a lot

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:53.329
of random noise to these images, they get
quite difficult to recognize. Let's engage

00:01:53.329 --> 00:02:00.189
in modesty and say that I, myself, as a human
can recognize approximately half of them,

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:05.499
but only if I look closely and maybe even
squint. A neural network can guess this correctly

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:11.030
approximately 50% of the time as well, which
is a quite respectable result.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:16.540
Therefore, adding random noise is not really
fooling the neural networks. However, if you

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.370
look at these adversarial examples in the
even columns, you see how carefully they are

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.540
crafted as they look very similar to the original
images, but the classification accuracy of

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:34.209
the neural network on these examples is 0%.
You heard it correctly. It gets it wrong basically all

00:02:34.209 --> 00:02:35.409
the time.

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:40.900
The take home message is that carefully crafted
adversarial examples can be used to fool deep

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:46.290
neural network reliably. You can watch them
flounder on many hilarious examples to your

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:56.840
enjoyment. "My dear sir, the Queen wears a
shower cap you say? I beg your pardon?"

